description:
2026-09-11, from the op-mzvp.3 Haiku dogfood (fresh agent, docs only, no source). Its top-ranked finding, rated HIGH, and it reproduces independently what I hit building examples/fhr_ring_rpt_endf.rs by hand.

THE ASYMMETRY: pebble_beds::fhr_pebble gives you fhr_pebble_geometry() for the ring-RPT pebble — one call, concentric shells, done. There is NO equivalent for the explicit-TRISO pebble. The crate supplies the PARTS (pack_spheres/PackedSpheres, TrisoSpec, triso_layer_at, build_triso_particle) but no assembly, so every caller must hand-write the same closure:

    |p| { let r = p.norm();
          if r < R_FUEL_ZONE { match packed.containing_center(p) {
              Some(c) => layer_material(triso_layer_at((p-c).norm(), &spec)...),
              None => matrix } }
          else { ...shell/coolant... } }

The dogfood agent got the ring-RPT pebble working and left the explicit one structurally incomplete, reporting 'the document describes the concept but doesn't provide a single function'. Per the crate's own 'if it is too complex for Haiku, it is a bad API' rule, that is the finding.

PROPOSAL: build_explicit_triso_pebble(packed, spec, layer_materials, r_fuel_zone, r_shell_outer, r_root, matrix_mat, coolant_mat, temperature) returning the same shape fhr_pebble_geometry does — or, if a closure is the right interface for delta tracking, a documented explicit_triso_material_at() helper that returns the closure. Either removes the copy-paste and makes the explicit and RPT paths symmetric, which is the whole point of a study that compares them.

SECOND, RELATED, also HIGH in the dogfood: no worked CSG example anywhere showing how to go from PackedSpheres to a geometry, or how to build concentric shells. Callers reverse-engineer from signatures.

Cross-ref op-mzvp.3, op-mzvp.3.1, op-mzvp.3.2.
